1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,610
One link. A whole company shares it.

2
00:00:03,650 --> 00:00:06,390
Lunch hour. Everyone wants it at once.

3
00:00:06,430 --> 00:00:08,180
Something has to slow down.

4
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:10,300
So… who would YOU slow down?

5
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:18,050
Network slicing splits one network into
reserved virtual lanes, called slices.

6
00:00:18,090 --> 00:00:23,460
We built a real testbed: 7 departments, 7 slices, one 1-megabit link.

7
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:28,000
950 kilobits are usable, but 1,480 were promised.

8
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:31,490
That's normal, like a gym selling more memberships than machines.

9
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:35,190
Normal networks decide with technical labels, set at install time.

10
00:00:35,230 --> 00:00:42,030
The canteen screens are tagged URLLC, premium.
The regulated quality lab, eMBB, standard.

11
00:00:42,070 --> 00:00:45,380
So the network slows the lab… and protects the lunch menu.

12
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,700
Our software ignores labels and asks business questions.

13
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:51,870
How critical is it? What does a minute of outage cost?

14
00:00:51,910 --> 00:00:57,150
Factory line: £8,400. Canteen screens: £15.

15
00:00:57,190 --> 00:01:03,380
Safety rules? Customer impact?
How long can it cope? Was it slowed recently?

16
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,370
Then a 7-step loop runs every 2 seconds.

17
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:10,510
1. Measure real traffic, delay and lost data at the gateway.

18
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:15,430
2. Detect: demand over 102% of the link, twice in a row.

19
00:01:15,470 --> 00:01:17,010
One spike can't fool it.

20
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:21,630
3. Score: each slice gets a business score, 0 to 100.

21
00:01:21,670 --> 00:01:27,570
4. Decide. Many small cuts beat one big cut.
Harm grows faster than the cut.

22
00:01:27,610 --> 00:01:35,440
One slice cut 60%: harm 0.36. Six slices cut 10%: just 0.06.

23
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:39,800
Life-safety slices are never touched.
No slice drops below its minimum.

24
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:43,000
5. Apply the new speed limits on the gateway.

25
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:46,560
6. Restore bandwidth after each recovery window.

26
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:52,650
7. Record a permanent explanation.
Nobody can edit or delete it, not even an admin.

27
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:58,910
In the lunch-hour rush, demand hit 1,316 kilobits, with 950 usable.

28
00:01:58,950 --> 00:02:03,310
With a safety margin, it had to free 251.5 kilobits.

29
00:02:03,350 --> 00:02:07,270
Remember our question? The canteen screens took an 80% cut.

30
00:02:07,310 --> 00:02:14,350
Office Wi-Fi lost 32%, the warehouse 24%, CCTV 12%.
Patient monitoring: never cut.

31
00:02:14,390 --> 00:02:20,250
Factory and lab: untouched.
The gateway enforced it 4 seconds later, with no button pressed.

32
00:02:20,670 --> 00:02:25,280
What's new?
Not slicing, not the sharing maths, but the combination.

33
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:29,770
Business value, not labels. Small cuts, not one deep cut.

34
00:02:29,810 --> 00:02:32,120
Safety as a hard rule, not a weight.

35
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:35,370
And it is proved on real traffic, every 2 seconds recorded.

36
00:02:35,410 --> 00:02:41,790
With arbitration off, the clinic waited half a second.
On: 23 milliseconds, no loss.

37
00:02:42,210 --> 00:02:46,300
Today the link is real but small:
1 megabit, shaped in our own software.

38
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:53,100
Real traffic, measured delay, phones on a
hotspot, and the full engine. 460 tests pass.

39
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:56,085
Next: seven phones measured, kernel shaping,

40
00:02:56,125 --> 00:02:58,930
a real 5G core, and radio slicing.

41
00:02:59,250 --> 00:03:02,560
When the link is full, it slows down
what matters least to the business,

42
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:07,220
in small, fair, safe steps. And it explains every decision.
